{
  "image_name": "2b5c3abc547cbd0b13086e40aa3c2f5c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes gene-therapy strategies for adrenal progenitor cells and the expected tissue-level effects in two adrenal disorders. At the top, three therapeutic routes are contrasted: direct in vivo delivery of gene-editing components in lipid nanoparticles to a diseased progenitor cell; ex vivo autologous therapy, where patient-derived iPSCs are gene edited, differentiated, and reimplanted; and allogeneic therapy, where healthy-donor iPSCs are differentiated into progenitors and implanted into the patient. In each case, the goal is to replace or convert diseased progenitors into corrected ones.\n\nThe lower part shows how this correction could restore adrenal cortex organization over time. For congenital adrenal hyperplasia, the diagrams suggest progressive normalization of the outer zona glomerulosa and middle zona fasciculata, with increasing numbers of healthy differentiated cells after therapy. For primary adrenal insufficiency, a broader recovery is depicted, including restoration across zona glomerulosa, fasciculata, and reticularis. A side legend distinguishes diseased versus healthy progenitor cells and their differentiated descendants. Overall, the figure conveys that repaired or donor-derived adrenal progenitors could repopulate adrenal zones and gradually rebuild functional adrenal tissue architecture.",
